Fixed. `pip` works again.

**What was wrong:** the `pip` package directory itself was missing from `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/` — only the orphaned `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata was left behind. So `/usr/local/bin/pip3` existed and ran, but died on `from pip._internal.cli.main import main` with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`. Nothing was wrong with your PATH, proxies, or pip config (there is no pip.conf anywhere); the script was fine, the module it imports was gone.

**Why the obvious fixes didn't work:** `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` bails out with "Requirement already satisfied: pip in ... (24.3.1)" — it trusts the stale metadata and never restores the files. It's worth knowing, because it looks like it succeeded.

**The fix I applied:** installed the bundled wheel directly, forcing the reinstall and skipping the metadata check:

```
python3 /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl/pip \
  install --force-reinstall --no-index --no-cache-dir \
  /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl
```

That runs pip's zipimport entry point from inside the wheel, so it works without a working pip, and needs no network.

**Verified:** `pip3 --version` and `python3 -m pip --version` both report 24.3.1 from `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip`; `import pip` resolves; and a real install end to end (`pip3 install --no-cache-dir --target /tmp/pipcheck six`) succeeded and the installed package imported. The temp target is cleaned up; nothing was left behind in the repo.

Two things I noticed but did not change: your `/usr/bin/python3` (3.11.2, the Debian one) has no pip either — that's normal for Debian, which ships it as a separate `python3-pip` package, so `pip3 install` there fails independently of this bug. And an empty `.gnomon-audit/` log plus `.gnomon/` were already in this directory; not mine to touch.

